\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Rotational ambiguity in unfolded ring projections is invisible to layout-regularity self-assessment.} (a)--(c)~The same physical ring, unfolded with three different starting angles $\theta $. All three projections satisfy every internal metric (spacing uniformity, boundary continuity, mask completeness, single-keystone presence) and produce visually clean segmentations, but the mapping from image columns to physical lining components depends on $\theta $, which is not directly observable from the image. (d)~The proposed labelling layer fixes this ambiguity: the keystone is localised first, then all remaining blocks receive canonical class identifiers by signed offset from the keystone. Cell colours and labels are illustrative schematics, not actual depth-map renders.}}{3}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:rotation}{{1}{3}{\textbf {Rotational ambiguity in unfolded ring projections is invisible to layout-regularity self-assessment.} (a)--(c)~The same physical ring, unfolded with three different starting angles $\theta $. All three projections satisfy every internal metric (spacing uniformity, boundary continuity, mask completeness, single-keystone presence) and produce visually clean segmentations, but the mapping from image columns to physical lining components depends on $\theta $, which is not directly observable from the image. (d)~The proposed labelling layer fixes this ambiguity: the keystone is localised first, then all remaining blocks receive canonical class identifiers by signed offset from the keystone. Cell colours and labels are illustrative schematics, not actual depth-map renders}{figure.caption.3}{}}
